WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:00.880
Pat Verducci, Writing Coach, Teacher&nbsp;
and Mentor: And I think writers should&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:00.880 --> 00:00:02.640
challenge themselves like what's hard for you?&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:03.360 --> 00:00:07.600
Make yourself do that thing, make yourself&nbsp;
do it even if you're scared of doing it.

00:00:07.600 --> 00:00:13.840
Film Courage: When you are writing a story,&nbsp;
what type of structure do you use most often?

00:00:13.840 --> 00:00:22.240
Pat: Hero's Journey. I use that structure&nbsp;
probably the most because again it helps me&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:22.240 --> 00:00:27.760
track the emotions of my character. What are they&nbsp;
afraid of? What do they want? How do they have to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:27.760 --> 00:00:34.160
face that fear? I'm writing something now and&nbsp;
I decided to do something I should probably not&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:34.160 --> 00:00:42.880
have done which is like I'm going to go free! I’m&nbsp;
just to just start writing this thing and I just&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:44.000 --> 00:00:47.840
barfed it out which is actually good for me&nbsp;
because I need to actually do that because&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:47.840 --> 00:00:54.560
I tend to be over here in my left brain a lot.&nbsp;
I barfed this thing out and it's like 70 pages.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:54.560 --> 00:01:00.800
I'm not even really sure what it is except it&nbsp;
is a detective themed thing. But I read through&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:00.800 --> 00:01:07.200
it again and now I'm going Oh okay I think this&nbsp;
is what it is. This one little part over here,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:08.080 --> 00:01:16.640
that's where the juice lies and so now I'm taking&nbsp;
that little juicy thing and going okay how can&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:16.640 --> 00:01:24.080
I sort of approach the rewrite with more of a&nbsp;
map and a view to now what I think it's about.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:25.680 --> 00:01:32.640
Usually I make a very loose outline. It's not&nbsp;
detailed it really just says the major action&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:32.640 --> 00:01:37.920
beats. What happens in the inciting incident&nbsp;
or the call to adventure and whether the person&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:37.920 --> 00:01:44.000
pushes away from it out of fear. Then what&nbsp;
happens next to pull them in but I want to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:44.000 --> 00:01:49.826
leave it not detailed because then that leaves&nbsp;
things for me to discover when I'm writing. s

00:01:49.826 --> 00:01:54.320
Film Courage: Is it harder for you to not&nbsp;
be organized and invested in structure?

00:01:54.320 --> 00:01:59.520
Pat: Yes, it's very hard for me to be free. That's&nbsp;
why I think I gave myself that challenge of just&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:00.080 --> 00:02:12.000
Pat step away from the outline, step away! I&nbsp;
needed to just go blah. When I did that I actually&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:12.000 --> 00:02:15.360
found really interesting things&nbsp;
happening that I didn't expect&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:15.360 --> 00:02:22.320
and again that's where I found that little spark&nbsp;
that was like Oh, this is what it's about. For me&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:22.320 --> 00:02:26.960
(and I think writers should challenge themselves)&nbsp;
what's hard for you? Make yourself do that thing,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:27.680 --> 00:02:33.360
make yourself do it even if you're scared of&nbsp;
doing it. Make yourself do it and then get&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:33.360 --> 00:02:38.560
more comfortable with it because there's&nbsp;
something in there that's scaring you&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:38.560 --> 00:02:44.560
and that's good. You need to go there, you need to&nbsp;
go into your own approach to the cave as a writer.

00:02:47.040 --> 00:02:54.000
I work with a lot of people who are writing&nbsp;
memoir books (I work with authors as well).&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:54.880 --> 00:03:00.640
A lot of the times they're like I think I'm going&nbsp;
to change this so that it's not about the person&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:00.640 --> 00:03:07.200
so that when they and I'm like what this is your&nbsp;
draft no one's going to read it but you write&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:07.200 --> 00:03:13.840
every single thing that happened, write the truth&nbsp;
because if you're holding back we're going to know&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:14.640 --> 00:03:19.680
that you're being untruthful with us. Now after&nbsp;
you get out this version where everything (the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:19.680 --> 00:03:26.640
truth) is everything everybody did is on the page,&nbsp;
then you can go back and modulate and figure out,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:26.640 --> 00:03:33.520
but you have to tell the truth. If you were&nbsp;
writing a script that is based on experience&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:33.520 --> 00:03:38.400
and you find yourself going I don't want to write&nbsp;
that scene. You’ve got it, you’ve got to go there,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:39.520 --> 00:03:44.000
that's the scene, that's what your movie's&nbsp;
about. You're afraid to go there because&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:44.000 --> 00:03:46.320
there's something in there that&nbsp;
you just don't want to look at.

00:03:46.320 --> 00:03:55.280
Film Courage: For a right-brained writer who's&nbsp;
messy and chaotic and everything's sort of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:55.280 --> 00:04:02.560
based on intuition then what would scare them is&nbsp;
probably structure, rules, numbered sequences?

00:04:02.560 --> 00:04:07.120
Pat: Right and and I’ve worked with&nbsp;
a lot of right-brained writers and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:10.880 --> 00:04:16.480
it's not like they do it 180 and suddenly&nbsp;
become like I'm have detailed outlines.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:16.480 --> 00:04:23.840
Usually it's just getting them to a place&nbsp;
that is comfortable for them to organize&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:23.840 --> 00:04:29.200
something so that they don't get lost in the&nbsp;
weeds. Their right brain is their strength&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:29.200 --> 00:04:35.680
it's like their superpower, but it can also lead&nbsp;
them into the darkness and down tangents and out&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:35.680 --> 00:04:39.760
into the woods and then they don't know how&nbsp;
to get back and oh my where's the flashlight!&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:41.600 --> 00:04:48.320
Helping them find something that's comfortable&nbsp;
in terms of organization but not too detailed.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:50.400 --> 00:04:55.680
Sometimes it's even just a matter of talking&nbsp;
through the character's emotional journey and how&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:55.680 --> 00:05:01.120
they change that gives them enough in their head&nbsp;
to guide their writing as their writing. Maybe&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:01.120 --> 00:05:06.640
they don't even write an outline, they just talk&nbsp;
it through so that they know where they're going.

00:05:06.640 --> 00:05:10.080
Film Courage: I wouldn't know anything about it.

00:05:10.080 --> 00:05:11.520
Pat: Is that your…

00:05:11.520 --> 00:05:14.960
Film Courage: I'm your right brain tried and true.

00:05:14.960 --> 00:05:17.360
Pat: Check out The Hero's Journey.

00:05:17.360 --> 00:05:23.120
Film Courage: Okay yeah. I love the interview&nbsp;
that Bill Moyers did with Joseph Campbell. It’s&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:23.680 --> 00:05:32.000
excellent. The two of them together were&nbsp;
a great team in terms of the interview.

00:05:32.000 --> 00:05:36.560
Pat: That's a great piece. I'm so&nbsp;
glad they recorded it for posterity.

00:05:36.560 --> 00:05:41.040
Film Courage: Wouldn't you say that if writers&nbsp;
and filmmakers follow these structures that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:41.040 --> 00:05:46.560
their films will be predictable? That's often&nbsp;
something we get in our comments “This is why&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:47.200 --> 00:05:50.840
Hollywood produces these formulaic movies.”

00:05:50.840 --> 00:05:58.160
Pat: Yes, yes…that's always the danger, that's&nbsp;
always the danger. You know when you're watching&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:58.160 --> 00:06:07.120
a formulaic movie, you know exactly what's going&nbsp;
to happen in the climax on 10 minutes, you know&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:07.680 --> 00:06:15.360
that movie is going to fulfill those expectations.&nbsp;
There's a couple of ways to approach that idea of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:15.360 --> 00:06:23.680
cliché and predictability. If you're writing the&nbsp;
predictability doesn't just lie along structure&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:23.680 --> 00:06:30.640
lines, it also lies along genre lines, that&nbsp;
certain genres hold have conventions that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:30.640 --> 00:06:38.720
become familiar patterns. The trick is to look at&nbsp;
these forms whether it's a structural form like&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:38.720 --> 00:06:44.640
The Hero's Journey or three-act structure as a&nbsp;
form and not a formula that it's just there to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:44.640 --> 00:06:51.760
give you some landmarks to help work through the&nbsp;
story with your character. Then if you're writing&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:51.760 --> 00:06:59.440
in a specific genre if I'm writing a horror&nbsp;
movie and it doesn't have scary stuff in it like&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:59.440 --> 00:07:04.800
that's going to be a failure I have to understand&nbsp;
the genre I'm working in and what the expectations&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:04.800 --> 00:07:11.040
of my audience are and I have to deliver that in&nbsp;
a way that feels fresh. The audience is coming to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:11.040 --> 00:07:16.720
your movie with expectations of the conventions&nbsp;
of that genre, they're going to want to see in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:16.720 --> 00:07:22.960
the romantic comedy the mistaken identity or the&nbsp;
misheard thing or the kiss at the end or whatever.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:23.520 --> 00:07:30.560
How do you deliver those things without making&nbsp;
them feel like boring and clichéd and well trod.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:31.680 --> 00:07:38.080
That's your challenge as a writer to either&nbsp;
embrace the conventions and make them feel fresh&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:38.080 --> 00:07:44.960
or subvert them. I just watched a movie last week.&nbsp;
It's not a great movie but it's good and it's very&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:44.960 --> 00:07:52.640
like lovely. The Mitchells Versus The Machines on&nbsp;
Netflix. It's an animated movie and one thing they&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:52.640 --> 00:07:57.360
did at the ending which I thought was really&nbsp;
funny is in The Hero's Journey the way that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:57.360 --> 00:08:02.480
they combated this idea of like And now this is&nbsp;
going to happen, and now this is going to happen!&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:03.520 --> 00:08:08.720
What happens is in The Hero's Journey in the&nbsp;
climax when you're facing off against the bad guy.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:08.720 --> 00:08:15.280
It looks like you're going to die. Oh no they died&nbsp;
but then they live! What they did in the movie is&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:16.000 --> 00:08:21.520
they face the robots and the family's fighting&nbsp;
and they beat the robots and it's like they&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:21.520 --> 00:08:26.640
destroy the bad guy and it's like yay, but then&nbsp;
the main character has fallen into this pit&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:27.280 --> 00:08:31.680
and the family gathers around it's like oh my&nbsp;
goodness Katie! No! And it looks like she's&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:31.680 --> 00:08:38.000
dead and she just pops up, she goes “Just&nbsp;
kidding.” They're mocking that story moment&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:38.800 --> 00:08:44.560
in The Hero’s Journey and so they're acknowledging&nbsp;
it and they're making fun of it and we laugh.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:45.680 --> 00:08:52.720
There's ways of embracing things at the same time&nbsp;
that you're going Yeah I know what I'm doing.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:52.720 --> 00:09:01.680
I know that that is that moment I'm going to&nbsp;
have fun with it so I think you have to find&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:02.320 --> 00:09:14.960
your way toward originality. Some people say&nbsp;
there's movies that don't embody some sort of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:14.960 --> 00:09:21.920
three-act structure which is absolutely true.&nbsp;
There are films that are mini-plot or anti-plot&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:21.920 --> 00:09:27.920
but the thing that people have to understand&nbsp;
is that mini-plot and anti-plot are a response&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:27.920 --> 00:09:35.840
to arch-plot which is three-act structure. They&nbsp;
are responses to the classical form so they take&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:35.840 --> 00:09:43.360
elements of three-act structure and twist them and&nbsp;
use them in different ways. In a mini-plot movie&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:47.040 --> 00:09:51.680
you might get all this information except&nbsp;
what you're used to getting in a normal&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:51.680 --> 00:09:57.360
movie. They're messing with your expectations of&nbsp;
information that you get about the character or&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:58.080 --> 00:10:05.120
the main character in the mini-plot play or&nbsp;
the anti-plot play or movie may not change but&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:05.120 --> 00:10:10.640
the audience has a shift, has a transformation.&nbsp;
They're taking these elements and turning them&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:10.640 --> 00:10:15.360
around and using them in different ways&nbsp;
but in order to do that successfully you&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:15.360 --> 00:10:20.800
have to actually understand arch-plot. For&nbsp;
those of you who are interested in doing&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:20.800 --> 00:10:25.040
really cool weird things with structure,&nbsp;
just learn what classical structure&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:25.040 --> 00:10:32.640
is and then think about how you're subverting it&nbsp;
or playing with it, or commenting on it because&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:33.200 --> 00:10:38.480
that's where really interesting stuff starts to&nbsp;
happen structurally in dramatic storytelling.

00:10:38.480 --> 00:10:40.720
Film Courage: Can you think&nbsp;
of a movie that does this?

00:10:40.720 --> 00:10:48.560
Pat: Tarantino does that all the time.&nbsp;
He plays around with these conventions&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:49.200 --> 00:10:56.960
of ways that we're normally used to seeing movies&nbsp;
and he just messes with them. He makes chapters,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:56.960 --> 00:11:03.920
he flips time zones, people are commenting, we&nbsp;
circle back, where are we? He does a lot of fun&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:03.920 --> 00:11:11.120
with structure because he understands what&nbsp;
he's playing with. If people are interested&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:11.120 --> 00:11:21.920
in Jim Jarmusch also creates movies that are not&nbsp;
the normal structure that we're used to. These are&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:21.920 --> 00:11:30.080
people who are doing like really cool experimental&nbsp;
stuff but they also understand movies, all kinds&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:30.080 --> 00:11:35.600
of movies and that's why they're so successful&nbsp;
breaking the rules and subverting the conventions.

00:11:36.480 --> 00:11:40.400
Film Courage: So that old adage Before you can&nbsp;
break the rules, you have to know the rules?

00:11:40.400 --> 00:11:45.520
Pat: That's right, I believe that.

00:11:45.520 --> 00:11:47.033
What story structure do you prefer?

